Proposed changes to tools/recomp/rt, from the proof work of 2026-10-13
(docs/issues/native-proof.md, R2.6 and R2.7).  Not applied: tools/recomp/rt has a keeper.
Made against rt/ as of 2026-10-12 23:48 (runtime.cpp) and 16:11 (vu0.h); apply from the
repository root with `patch -p1 < tools/recomp/proof/rt-proposed.patch`.

1. runtime.cpp: `g_vu0` loses `static`.
   The proof's instrumented runtime (tools/recomp/proof/proof_rt.cpp) records the vector
   unit's registers, microprogram and data memory with every machine state it captures, so
   that a function that uses the unit (anim_buildPose, gfx_uploadMicrocode, sceGsSyncPath,
   0x34f890 - the only four in the executable) can be replayed against the interpreter.
   It needs to read the runtime's unit.  Nothing else changes; the proof builds from a
   snapshot in which this one word is already removed.

2. vu0.h: the unit's numbers.
   What the header does today with an operand whose exponent field is 255 (an infinity or
   a NaN to the host) or 0 with a fraction (a denormal) is the host's own arithmetic:
   - a NaN result carries the host's sign bit (ARM64 0x7fc00000, x86-64 0xffc00000), and
     each host hands an operand's NaN on its own way: two builds of the header disagree;
   - it is not what the emulator computes either.  Measured in the rig (rigvu.py, 14
     instructions x 16,384 operand pairs): the header differs from the emulator on 15,479
     of 229,376 results - 260 with a denormal operand (MAX/MINI), 1,888 with an infinity,
     13,331 with a NaN - and on none with finite normal operands.
   The console's unit has no infinity, no NaN and no denormal.  The change brings what an
   arithmetic instruction reads into that range first (exponent 255: the largest number of
   its sign; exponent 0: zero), makes a result too small to be a number zero, and lets MAX
   and MINI compare the two bit patterns as sign and magnitude and hand one on unchanged -
   the rule tools/recomp/render/vu1_step.h already follows for VU1.  With it:
   - the result no longer depends on the host: the operands are finite, the host rounds
     toward zero, so no infinity and no NaN can arise (arith.py vu still passes on finite
     operands; vu0ref.py's 'console' model is the written reference, 0 differences from
     the header on the 229,376 results above);
   - against the emulator 5,120 results still differ, all with an infinity or NaN operand:
     2,816 in ADD (the emulator clamps the first operand of ADD only: Play! 83700b2c,
     Source/ee/VUShared.cpp, ADD_base) and 2,304 in FTOI of a NaN (the emulator converts
     with the host's instruction: 0).  SUB, MUL, MAX, MINI, ITOF, ABS: 0.  The emulator's
     own rule is per instruction (ADDA, SUBA, MSUB and MSUBA are not clamped at all
     there), so it cannot be both followed exactly and made independent of the host.
   - operands of that kind do reach the game's own program in play: a measurement build
     counted them over four recorded sessions (h6w, s2w, s3w, s5w; 2.8 thousand million
     operands read by arithmetic in 328 million microprogram instructions) - 0.55 % had
     exponent 255, 1.0 % were denormal, the same shares in every session.  They sit in
     fields whose results the game does not read back: with the change the tick log of
     each of the four sessions is byte for byte the log without it, and an image of all
     32 MB taken at the same tick with and without is identical.  MAX and MINI are not
     executed by the game's program at all.

